            0.399,
            0.55
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "Think and Reflect"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.128,
            0.565,
            0.855,
            0.611
          ],
          "content": "Try to prove the irrationality of $\\sqrt { 3 }$ using the approach of proof by contradiction. Will the same approach work for ${ \\sqrt { 5 } }   ,   { \\sqrt { 7 } }$ , or 10 ?"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.114,
            0.647,
            0.523,
            0.671
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "3.5.2 Construction of Length $\\sqrt { \\mathrm { n } }$"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.131,
            0.691,
            0.399,
            0.714
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "Think and Reflect"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.128,
            0.728,
            0.854,
            0.768
          ],
          "content": "We have seen how to obtain a line whose length is a rational number. How do we obtain lines whose lengths are irrational?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.113,
            0.784,
            0.885,
            0.826
          ],
          "content": "For example, to construct a line segment of length $\\sqrt { 2 }$ and mark its position on the number line, we may proceed as follows."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.113,
            0.83,
            0.885,
            0.871
          ],
          "content": "**Step 1:** On the number line (see Fig. 3.11), we measure OA = 1 unit and draw a perpendicular on OA through A."
        }
      ]
    }
